    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Example"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "text",
      "code": "n = 1  ->  [\"()\"]\nn = 2  ->  [\"(())\", \"()()\"]\nn = 3  ->  [\"((()))\", \"(()())\", \"(())()\", \"()(())\", \"()()()\"]"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "The idea: only make choices that keep the prefix valid"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Generating all 2²ⁿ strings of brackets and keeping the valid ones works, but most of them are hopeless from an early point — anything starting with `)`, for example. Backtracking can **prune** those branches before they grow: build the string one bracket at a time and only allow a move that keeps the prefix fixable."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "Two counts are enough. A `(` is allowed while fewer than `n` have been used. A `)` is allowed only while it has something to close — while `close < open`. With those rules every prefix stays valid, so every string that reaches length 2n is an answer, and nothing needs checking at the end."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "1. Brute force: every bracket string, then filter"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function generateParenthesis(n) {\n  const result = [];\n  const valid = (s) => {\n    let balance = 0;\n    for (const ch of s) {\n      balance += ch === \"(\" ? 1 : -1;\n      if (balance < 0) return false;          // a ')' with nothing to close\n    }\n    return balance === 0;\n  };\n  const all = (s) => {                        // every string of '(' and ')' of length 2n\n    if (s.length === 2 * n) {\n      if (valid(s)) result.push(s);\n      return;\n    }\n    all(s + \"(\");\n    all(s + \")\");\n  };\n  all(\"\");\n  return result;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def generate_parenthesis(n):\n    result = []\n\n    def valid(s):\n        balance = 0\n        for ch in s:\n            balance += 1 if ch == \"(\" else -1\n            if balance < 0:\n                return False              # a ')' with nothing to close\n        return balance == 0\n\n    def every(s):                         # every string of '(' and ')' of length 2n\n        if len(s) == 2 * n:\n            if valid(s):\n                result.append(s)\n            return\n        every(s + \"(\")\n        every(s + \")\")\n\n    every(\"\")\n    return result"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "List<String> generateParenthesis(int n) {\n    List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();\n    every(\"\", n, result);\n    return result;\n}\n\nvoid every(String s, int n, List<String> result) {   // every string of '(' and ')' of length 2n\n    if (s.length() == 2 * n) {\n        if (valid(s)) result.add(s);\n        return;\n    }\n    every(s + \"(\", n, result);\n    every(s + \")\", n, result);\n}\n\nboolean valid(String s) {\n    int balance = 0;\n    for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) {\n        balance += ch == '(' ? 1 : -1;\n        if (balance < 0) return false;           // a ')' with nothing to close\n    }\n    return balance == 0;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "bool valid(const string& s) {\n    int balance = 0;\n    for (char ch : s) {\n        balance += ch == '(' ? 1 : -1;\n        if (balance < 0) return false;           // a ')' with nothing to close\n    }\n    return balance == 0;\n}\n\nvoid every(const string& s, int n, vector<string>& result) {   // every string of length 2n\n    if ((int)s.size() == 2 * n) {\n        if (valid(s)) result.push_back(s);\n        return;\n    }\n    every(s + \"(\", n, result);\n    every(s + \")\", n, result);\n}\n\nvector<string> generateParenthesis(int n) {\n    vector<string> result;\n    every(\"\", n, result);\n    return result;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(2²ⁿ · n), space O(n)** beyond the output. For n = 8 that's 65,536 strings to build and check, to keep 1,430."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "2. Backtracking with two counts"
    },
    {
      "type": "simulator",
      "program": "generate-parentheses",
      "caption": "'(' while some are left, ')' only to close one: every finished string is valid."
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "javascript",
      "code": "function generateParenthesis(n) {\n  const result = [];\n  const path = [];\n  const build = (open, close) => {            // how many '(' and ')' are used so far\n    if (path.length === 2 * n) {\n      result.push(path.join(\"\"));\n      return;\n    }\n    if (open < n) {                           // a '(' is allowed while some are left\n      path.push(\"(\");\n      build(open + 1, close);\n      path.pop();\n    }\n    if (close < open) {                       // a ')' only if it closes something\n      path.push(\")\");\n      build(open, close + 1);\n      path.pop();\n    }\n  };\n  build(0, 0);\n  return result;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "python",
      "code": "def generate_parenthesis(n):\n    result = []\n    path = []\n\n    def build(open_, close):              # how many '(' and ')' are used so far\n        if len(path) == 2 * n:\n            result.append(\"\".join(path))\n            return\n        if open_ < n:                     # a '(' is allowed while some are left\n            path.append(\"(\")\n            build(open_ + 1, close)\n            path.pop()\n        if close < open_:                 # a ')' only if it closes something\n            path.append(\")\")\n            build(open_, close + 1)\n            path.pop()\n\n    build(0, 0)\n    return result"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "java",
      "code": "List<String> generateParenthesis(int n) {\n    List<String> result = new ArrayList<>();\n    build(new StringBuilder(), 0, 0, n, result);\n    return result;\n}\n\nvoid build(StringBuilder path, int open, int close, int n, List<String> result) {\n    if (path.length() == 2 * n) {\n        result.add(path.toString());\n        return;\n    }\n    if (open < n) {                              // a '(' is allowed while some are left\n        path.append('(');\n        build(path, open + 1, close, n, result);\n        path.deleteCharAt(path.length() - 1);\n    }\n    if (close < open) {                          // a ')' only if it closes something\n        path.append(')');\n        build(path, open, close + 1, n, result);\n        path.deleteCharAt(path.length() - 1);\n    }\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "code",
      "language": "cpp",
      "code": "void build(string& path, int open, int close, int n, vector<string>& result) {\n    if ((int)path.size() == 2 * n) {\n        result.push_back(path);\n        return;\n    }\n    if (open < n) {                              // a '(' is allowed while some are left\n        path.push_back('(');\n        build(path, open + 1, close, n, result);\n        path.pop_back();\n    }\n    if (close < open) {                          // a ')' only if it closes something\n        path.push_back(')');\n        build(path, open, close + 1, n, result);\n        path.pop_back();\n    }\n}\n\nvector<string> generateParenthesis(int n) {\n    vector<string> result;\n    string path;\n    build(path, 0, 0, n, result);\n    return result;\n}"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "**Time O(4ⁿ / √n), space O(n)** beyond the output. The number of answers is the n-th **Catalan number**, about 4ⁿ / (n^1.5 · √π), and since no branch dead-ends, the work is that number times the length 2n."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Comparing the approaches"
    },
    {
      "type": "complexity",
      "rows": [
        {
          "approach": "Brute force: every bracket string, then filter",
          "time": "O(2²ⁿ · n)",
          "timeWhy": "All **2²ⁿ** strings of length 2n, each checked in O(n).",
          "space": "O(n)",
          "spaceWhy": "One string at a time (besides the output)."
        },
        {
          "approach": "Backtracking with two counts",
          "time": "O(4ⁿ / √n)",
          "timeWhy": "Only **valid prefixes** are built: the Catalan number of answers times their length.",
          "space": "O(n)",
          "spaceWhy": "One path of **2n** brackets and the call stack."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "text": "The lesson of this problem is **pruning**: the rules about when a choice is allowed cut the search down to exactly the answers."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Edge cases to test"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**n = 1** — just `\"()\"`.",
        "**Count check** — n = 3 gives 5 answers, n = 4 gives 14: the Catalan numbers.",
        "**Using `close < n` instead of `close < open`** — it allows `\")(\"`-style prefixes and produces invalid strings.",
        "**No duplicates** — each answer is built along exactly one path, so none appears twice."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "text": "Variations"
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": false,
      "items": [
        "**Letter Combinations of a Phone Number** — backtracking where every choice is allowed. Previous lesson.",
        "**Remove Invalid Parentheses** — backtracking over which brackets to delete, pruned with the same balance counts.",
        "**Different Ways to Add Parentheses** — split an expression at each operator and combine the results of both sides."
      ]
    }
  ],
